codebase-atlas.evaluation:
The success fixture checks:
- core-first ordering, with loosely spelled paths matched;
- the byte-exact atlas and its receipt;
- map edges equal to the cards' dependsOn, where the CLI -> API edge appears only after the repair and is absent from the draft the critic audited;
- two complaints about one card merged into one repair, and an added CI section;
- the uncovered areas;
- read-only roles: no write tool anywhere, a synthesizer with no tools at all, and a read-only Codex critic;
- only docs/ left new in the checkout.

The modes cover:
- an existing out (refused before any agent call);
- replace with an approving critic (complete, with the previous digest recorded);
- a degraded run: an unmatched subsystem, a capped one, a failed reader, an invented key file dropped from the document and the start-here enum, and a repair group past the cap;
- a failed critic, where the draft is written;
- an empty survey.

F3 kills after the write and before its checkpoint. The fixtures prove the accounting and the assembly, not that real readers write accurate cards. Repairs are not re-reviewed. An existing out that ctx.readFile cannot snapshot (over 1 MiB, not UTF-8) is treated as absent and refused only at the final exclusive write.

dependency-upgrade.evaluation:
The success fixture upgrades a fake tinyclock 1 to 2. The researcher's prompt carries code's lockfile evidence, and the analyst's prompt lists the three code-found files. The upgrader's worktree fixes one caller. In round 0 the stand-in install refreshes the lockfile and the real verification command exits 1. The triager runs in that checkout. One fixer repairs the other caller in a worktree of the integrated commit (its reply asserts that the upgrader's change is there), and the cluster naming target.mjs gets no fixer. Round 1 passes. The checkout then holds exactly the manifest, the lockfile and both callers, in one commit on the base. The approved details, digest and file list match the plan JSON the upgrader received, and only the two writers had edit tools. Modes cover:
- reconsider (nothing attempted);
- a declined approval;
- an upgrader refused for touching target.mjs;
- a failing fixer (journaled; the loop stops and reports the triager's clusters);
- maxFixRounds 0 with a failing triage (settled; the failing command remains);
- the round cap: in round 0 three fixers run and a fourth cluster is deferred; round 1 has a report-only triage, and the run ends unstable with its root cause;
- a fixer refused for touching target.mjs;
- an install that writes an unplanned file (refused before publication);
- a checkout that moves during research or during a fixer (base-moved before any writer or the next round; nothing published).
The triage and the fixers are settled calls, so the module records any reply that throws and fails its checks on it. F3 kills the upgrader after its edit. The install and test commands are node stand-ins: no package manager, registry or web research runs, and nothing shows migration quality. Three things go untested: the publish-time base-moved check, the remaining check-then-act window, and a verification command (rather than the install) that writes files.

refactor-campaign.evaluation:
The success fixture checks the following:
- One target is deferred, and the clock cannot be pinned.
- A two-file net that code proves green is published.
- The baseline runs the net and the verify command on the nets commit, and the approval subject carries those rows.
- The approved digest is the hash of the goal-and-plan text the refactorer received, and that text parses back to the approved entries.
- The refactorer's prompt lists both net files as off limits.
- The review prompt holds the diff computed by code and the touched paths.
- The unmoved checkout fast-forwards to exactly the tree the final verification ran on.

Twenty-five modes cover:
- report-only, with a duplicate survey location dropped and counted;
- a red net rewritten in a fresh checkout from its full assertion output, with a golden file shown in part and counted;
- behavior changes caught by the net, by the ownership rule (a rewritten cases file) and by the reviewer;
- a renamed net, failed refactor and review calls, a refactorer that edits and then declines, one that changes nothing, and a declined plan;
- a verify command that is red only on the refactored tree, or only at the gate;
- a red baseline, where a net green alone fails beside another net's file;
- a dirty checkout at the start and at the publish;
- commits to the checkout while the nets are written (one conflicting with a net file) and while the refactor runs (one conflicting with it);
- two targets refactored one after the other, the second on the first's result behind both nets;
- a second refactor that breaks only the first target's net;
- a diff past its capture and a plan past the approval preview;
- refused nets: a collision, an edit to existing code, no behaviors, a missing declared file;
- no nets and no targets.

This round I ran 25 out-of-tree mutations: 15 new ones, plus 10 of the last round's 22 whose code changed. Each fails at least one check. Among them: an unsettled integration, an unsettled nets merge, a refusal always or never classed as moved, no pre-approval HEAD check, a baseline without the nets, a dirty check that throws or is missing, ignoring declined, and an excerpt that throws on overflow. F3 kills the run after the refactorer's isolated write.

What the fixtures do not show: the nets and refactors are scripted. So nothing shows that real nets pin enough behavior (nets are not mutation-tested), or that the reviewer catches what the nets miss. testCommand must accept a test file path as its last argument. A run-owned nets commit can be pruned along with its run. A commit that lands between the last HEAD check and the publish's preparation is still published onto (see the ctx.merge finding).

requirements-to-prd.evaluation:
The success fixture reads two files. Every lens and critic reply first checks that its prompt states the charter its step index names and no other, and check asserts this for both rounds. So a lens or critic routed to the wrong charter fails every contract that reaches it, and so does a misspelled branch literal, which workflow validate does not see. The fixture checks the code-assigned ids and quote attribution: case changes, an ellipsis and a typographic apostrophe still anchor, and an implied finding with no matching quote becomes inferred. It also checks the product context in the extract, draft and attack prompts and the code-added assumption in the critics' prompts. It checks the caps the draft and revision prompts state, the revision's raised by one per issue to 63 open questions and 43 assumptions. Every issue must reach the reviser with its fix, most severe first. The durable question's title, prompt, subject and details are checked too. One answered round redoes the PRD with findings that quote the answers, and the rendered PRD is published byte for byte. Modes cover:
- one failed critic (degraded), in which a quote with its parts out of order and a one-word quote stay inferred;
- every critic failing (unrevised, no revision);
- a failed lens in each round (degraded, with the first round's failure kept in asked);
- a failed revision with inline material, input answers and a committed PRD, whose re-run hint does not offer replace;
- material with nothing to extract;
- an empty extraction whose one unknown a question round asks, with the answer drafting the PRD;
- an answered round in which every lens fails (the first round's PRD stands and is written, degraded, with the answers in failedRedo);
- a blank file (no agent call);
- blank material with input answers, which the lenses read and a question round's answer joins;
- 59 pooled conflicts and unknowns (limited: every conflict and 31 unknowns listed, 19 unlisted);
- forty open questions left open (details cut with a count);
- an existing out, a stale replace digest, an existing out under publication 'commit' and an untracked material file under 'commit', each refused before any read or agent call with the files unchanged;
- a replace digest that redoes the PRD;
- a file appearing at out, and the file a replace digest named changing, while the agents work (the write fails against the checked baseline and leaves the other writer's bytes);
- three files over maxMaterialBytes together (the run fails with no agent call).

A scratch mutation runner made 13 changes, among them every lens or every critic given one charter, a misspelled branch literal in each of the two charter templates, no product context downstream, no fix in the revise prompt, no ifMatch, no clean-checkout check and a discarded earlier PRD. Each was caught, and the unchanged package passed all 20 contracts. F3 kills after the write and before its checkpoint; the resumed receipt still reports the null baseline. F1 and F2 land on whichever lens reaches the harness first, so the fixture's writer cites a finding that exists whichever lens fails. Six runs of each completed, F1 landing on three of the lenses and F2 on all four. A found quote proves the words occur in order, not that the finding reads them correctly. Not exercised: PRD quality, and whether a writer covers every listed conflict and unknown.
